I wrote the future-role review (the five-year durability check) to `docs/content-analysis/pipeline-logs/2026-11-01_07-06-25-887_tom-rhys-harries/review-future.json`. It passes `reviewSchema`, and every quoted passage matches the draft exactly. I didn't change any other file in the repo and didn't publish anything. The only other thing I created is a helper script in `/tmp/trh-future/`.

**Overall:** the main Type 6 claim would still hold in five years. Without the October 2026 *Clayface* press, his own words from 2015, 2020 and 2022 still support it, especially "fear is a big driving factor" and the motorbike line ("nothing subjective about whether an engine works"). The weak spots are the arguments against Type 3 and Type 7, which lean on release-week material.

**Findings (3 major, 3 minor):**
- **F1, major:** The draft calls the October 29 post-opening interview "the sharpest test the record offers." It is a single release-week interview, and the evidence record marks that claim stable even though the research notes say to keep watching it.
- **F2, major:** The case against Type 3 depends on one podcast line about the ACL ("for fear of losing this job"). Take that away and the draft offers no older evidence that rules out Three.
- **F3, major:** The evidence record tracks only one changing fact, the box-office figure. Several open questions have no recheck date:
  - "no one who has known him for twenty years has spoken on the record"
  - the "what would change this read" signals
  - an unreleased indie film he mentions having just shot
- **F4, minor:** The ending calls the opening "the biggest weekend of his life" and starts with "For now." Both will date badly.
- **F5, minor:** "That is why people are looking him up" is only true right now.
- **F6, minor:** The "Who is" FAQ says he "plays" the title role in *Clayface*; once the film is out of theatres it should say "played."

I marked five passages to protect, including how the draft reconciles the conflicting ACL accounts, the 2015 audition evidence, the engine line, and the "what would change this read" signals.

The repetition check flagged one phrase shared word-for-word with the Paul Bettany page, "the three types organized around fear." It's standard Enneagram wording rather than a reused argument, so I didn't count it as a finding. Contested typing stays flagged, and I found no new risks.

The two hashes in the output are copied from the request file, as the stage instructions say. They don't match a plain `shasum` of the files, which I assume is because the runner hashes a cleaned-up version.
